#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488201 :question/problem "Which of the following regions is connected?" :question/worked-solution "A region $D$ is connected if every pair of points in $D$ can be joined by a smooth curve or a path in $D.$\n\nAmong the given options, the only one that satisfies this definition is the following:\n\n![](images/08/0841ee8ea9f2d7454ee852041b3dfb470b6cf17237cec54960f400da337910c0.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488202 :question/problem "Which of the following is *not* a simply connected region?" :question/worked-solution "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/46/46305c070e2563b4dacf8b31c88c46b860f003b20ffdff608a5485c09a91bd11.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/a0/a0459ddbabbd810e43db084f5dd174437340914b53ed227f6ef7ce2e3a0fc383.png)\n\nAmong the given options, the only one that does not satisfy this definition is the following:  \n\n![](images/37/37597a2cecae00cca3eacec7265913070de9343be6afb9108644d6c8d0c1d93b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488220 :question/problem "Which of the following is *not* a simply connected region?" :question/worked-solution "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/46/46305c070e2563b4dacf8b31c88c46b860f003b20ffdff608a5485c09a91bd11.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/a0/a0459ddbabbd810e43db084f5dd174437340914b53ed227f6ef7ce2e3a0fc383.png)\n\nAmong the given options, the only one that does not satisfy this definition is the following: \n\n![](images/65/65e1c034f836802bb8127e754f0e1cb303144468b041d51da2ee1b8a89de1132.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488225 :question/problem "Which of the following regions are simply connected?\n\nI. $\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}|\\hspace{0.222em}y+2x\\ge 1\\}$\n\nII. $\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}|\\hspace{0.222em}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}|\\hspace{0.222em}y-2x\\ne 1\\}$" :question/worked-solution "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nLet's now examine our regions in turn.\n\n- Region I is simply connected. Indeed, it is the half-plane $y\\ge -2x+1.$ So,   it's a plane region without holes,\n\n- Region II is simply connected.  It's a plane region (a circle) without holes.\n\n- Region III is *not* simply connected. The region contains all the points of ${R}^{2}$ except those on the straight line $y=2x+1.$ Thus, the region is formed by two disjoint half-planes and, therefore, is not even a connected region.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488245 :question/problem "Which of the following regions is connected?" :question/worked-solution "A region $D$ is connected if every pair of points in $D$ can be joined by a smooth curve or a path in $D.$\n\nAmong the given options, the only one that satisfies this definition is the following:\n\n![](images/dd/dd767429c321d02dc6edf559127ff8ecc733bd5ecdf243b79f7b671fd78085c4.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488270 :question/problem "![](images/23/2304f9b9080a8b3ec4ff10284cee8e01ec8dff54fb879f7ed55dd7a3f80e2b23.png)\n\nWhich of the regions shown above are simply connected?" :question/worked-solution "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the regions:  \n\n- Region I is not simply connected. Instead, this is a region that consists of two pieces (it's not connected).\n\n- Region II is not simply connected. Instead, this is a connected region that contains a hole.\n\n- Region III is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n\n- Region IV is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III and IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490212 :question/problem "Which of the following represents the streamlines of the vector field $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=\\left(\\frac{1}{y-{x}^{2}}\\right)i+\\left(\\frac{2x}{y-{x}^{2}}\\right)j\n$$ over the domain $D=\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}y>{x}^{2}\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "The streamlines of a vector field are the level curves of its stream function and are tangential to the vector field at every point. \n\nIf $F$ represents the velocity field of a fluid flow, then they show the path traced out by a massless particle as it moves with the flow.\n\nA vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\left(\\frac{1}{y-{x}^{2}}\\right)i+\\left(\\frac{2x}{y-{x}^{2}}\\right)j\n$$\n\n is a vector field with simply connected domain $D,$ where $P(x,y)=\\frac{1}{y-{x}^{2}}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\frac{2x}{y-{x}^{2}}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=\\frac{2x}{(y-{x}^{2})^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-\\frac{2x}{(y-{x}^{2})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $F$ has a stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=\\frac{2x}{(y-{x}^{2})^{2}}+\\left(-\\frac{2x}{(y-{x}^{2})^{2}}\\right)=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we find a stream function. A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ if \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{y-{x}^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}=-\\frac{2x}{y-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating these equations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y-{x}^{2})+f(x),\\,ψ(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y-{x}^{2})+g(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are arbitrary functions. Finally, setting $f=g=0,$ we have\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y-{x}^{2}).\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the level curves of the stream function $ψ(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y-{x}^{2})$ given by the equations $ψ(x,y)=c.$\n\nNotice that the range of $ψ(x,y)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider level curves for any value of $c.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ψ(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(y-{x}^{2}) & =c \\\\ y-{x}^{2} & ={e}^{c} \\\\ y & ={x}^{2}+{e}^{c}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives us a family of parabolas with the vertex at $(0,{e}^{c}).$ Therefore, the correct graph (along with the vector field $F$) is as follows:\n\n![](images/ed/edc12146ffad4dc46ed6d57b3dfdc9a08ca92fb601ef5e08ed9ccef3a1c1da4c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490232 :question/problem "![](images/7d/7dad50d17444096c60b129131104209b8b7371d546f80d7b0c838baec1df8b4d.png)\n\nWhich of the given points lie on the line $y=-5x+6?$" :question/worked-solution "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(0,5),B(1,2)$ and $C(2,-4).$\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(0,5)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-5x+6 \\\\ (5) & =-5(0)+6 \\\\ 5 & =6\\hspace{0.278em}{\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does *not* lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(1,2)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-5x+6 \\\\ (2) & =-5(1)+6 \\\\ 2 & =1\\hspace{0.278em}{\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(2,-4)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-5x+6 \\\\ (-4) & =-5(2)+6 \\\\ -4 & =-4\\hspace{0.278em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only point $C$ lies on the line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490239 :question/problem "Which of the following graphs shows the line $y=-2x?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\n- For the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=0.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2x \\\\  & =-2(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is $(0,0).$\n\n- For the second point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=1.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2x \\\\  & =-2(1) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(1,-2).$\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of $y=-2x.$ \n\n![](images/c6/c632cc2770958b5d73f6e143de0b72d19b2099f9f656ff34f273d88acc4f9cb4.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490257 :question/problem "Copy and complete the table below and use it to determine which of the following is the graph of $y=2x+2.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, we fill the table. Given a value of $x$ we can find the corresponding $y$ by substituting $x$ into the formula $y=2x+2.$\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n- if $x=0$ then $y=2(0)+2=2,$\n\n- if $x=1$ then $y=2(1)+2=4,$\n\n- if $x=2$ then $y=2(2)+2=6,$ and\n\n- if $x=3$ then $y=2(3)+2=8.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n\nPlotting these $(x,y)$ pairs on the $xy$-plane: \n\n![](images/6b/6b2da4bfc7c768788d143999fb61e76bb3a60facffc35e430d98085b395932f7.png)\n\nFinally, connecting the points gives us a straight line. This is the graph of  $y=2x+2.$\n\n![](images/04/0400a1437f26b74feb16161cf51a88c175ee6837fe1e5d8f8785ba2bb2b9c68b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490279 :question/problem "![](images/f8/f886e9f5dfef693d1ea5d5a0a907a886be368549c1b9a4d43dce257d070e2fa2.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\nI. $y+3x=6$\n\nII. $\\frac{1}{3}y+x=2$\n\nIII. $y=-3x+6$" :question/worked-solution "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,6)$ and $(2,0)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/d6/d6cb6d026c181d79bda5c809729eb070c554c2025a287577df0330d1dd8a03e7.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation I: $y+3x=6.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,6)$ and $B(2,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6)+3(0) & =6 \\\\ 6 & =6\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓} \\\\ (0)+3(2) & =6 \\\\ 6 & =6\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y+3x=6$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation II: $\\frac{1}{3}y+x=2.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,6)$ and $B(2,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}(6)+(0) & =2 \\\\ 2+0 & =2 \\\\ 2 & =2\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(0)+(2) & =2 \\\\ 0+2 & =2 \\\\ 2 & =2\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,$\\frac{1}{3}y+x=2$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation III: $y=-3x+6.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,6)$ and $B(2,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6) & =-3(0)+6 \\\\ 6 & =6\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓} \\\\ (0) & =-3(2)+6 \\\\ 0 & =-6+6 \\\\ 0 & =0\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-3x+6$ is  an equation of the line in the graph.\n\nAll the given equations represented the line in the graph. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490285 :question/problem "Which of the following graphs shows the line $y=2-\\frac{1}{2}x?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\n- For the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=0.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2-\\frac{1}{2}x \\\\  & =2-\\frac{1}{2}(0) \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is $(0,2).$\n\n- For the second point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=8.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2-\\frac{1}{2}x \\\\  & =2-\\frac{1}{2}(8) \\\\  & =2-4 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(8,-2).$\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of $y=2-\\frac{1}{2}x.$\n\n![](images/a8/a8c562212687c848e2de25b757f59acd70467a085ae2e0713fbeafa355ad5bb5.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490287 :question/problem "![](images/71/716836dd3dfc93ac26bf9a960d5edcd04da96907b98c39e926a917b7b8feeeee.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\nI. $y=-2x+\\frac{1}{2}$\n\nII. $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2$\n\nIII. $y=\\frac{1}{2}x+2$" :question/worked-solution "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,2)$ and $(4,0)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/2b/2b9174d86d123228ae24fffeda4d2e4741e4a776f6671f7eff38340a59ba4d40.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation I: $y=-2x+\\frac{1}{2}.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,2)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2) & =-1(0)+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-2x+\\frac{1}{2}$ is *not* equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation II: $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,2)$ and $B(4,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2) & =-\\frac{1}{2}(0)+2 \\\\ 2 & =2\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓} \\\\ (0) & =-\\frac{1}{2}(4)+2 \\\\ 0 & =-2+2 \\\\ 0 & =0\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,$y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation III: $y=\\frac{1}{2}x+2.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,2)$ and $B(4,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2) & =\\frac{1}{2}(0)+2 \\\\ 2 & =2\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓} \\\\ (0) & =\\frac{1}{2}(4)+2 \\\\ 0 & =2+2 \\\\ 0 & =4\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=\\frac{1}{2}x+2$ is *not* equation of the line in the graph.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equation that represented the line in the graph was $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2,$ corresponding to answer choice II. Therefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490290 :question/problem "For the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}(\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix})⟶\\begin{bmatrix}uv \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\\left(\\frac{u}{v}\\right)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n  \n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x=uv$ and $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{u}{v}\\right).$ \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =u \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =\\frac{1}{u} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-\\frac{1}{v}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}v & u \\\\ \\frac{1}{u} & -\\frac{1}{v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =v\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{v}\\right)-u\\cdot \\frac{1}{u} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(1,3)}=-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490291 :question/problem "![](images/c3/c33968d9b5c86e39ba35c88a5f28bb8950178004c763a5c011633b9a060e98a8.png)\n\nIdentify the points that lie on the line $y=4x+2.$" :question/worked-solution "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(-1,-2)$, $B(0,1)$ and $C(1,6).$\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(-1,-2)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4x+2 \\\\ (-2) & =4(-1)+2 \\\\ -2 & =-2\\hspace{0.278em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(0,1)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4x+2 \\\\ (1) & =4(0)+2 \\\\ 1 & =2\\hspace{0.278em}{\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(1,6)$ results in true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4x+2 \\\\ (6) & =4(1)+2 \\\\ 6 & =6\\hspace{0.278em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only points $A$ and $C$ lie on the line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490304 :question/problem "Construct the vector field that has the stream function $ψ(x,y)={x}^{2}{e}^{1-y}.$" :question/worked-solution "A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ if the following equations hold:\n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}\n$$\n\nComputing the partial derivatives of the function $ψ(x,y),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{e}^{1-y}) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}{e}^{1-y}, \\\\ Q & =-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{e}^{1-y}) \\\\  & =-2x{e}^{1-y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field with the stream function $ψ(x,y)={x}^{2}{e}^{1-y}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & =P\\,i+Q\\,j \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}{e}^{1-y}\\,i-2x{e}^{1-y}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490314 :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields have a stream function?\n\nI. $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-x\\operatorname{cos}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$\n\nII. $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$\n\nIII. $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2{e}^{y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each vector field.\n\n- Vector field I doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II has a stream function. Indeed, the partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+(-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)=0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2{e}^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=1+2{e}^{y}\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490318 :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields have a stream function?\n\nI. $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)={x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$\n\nII. $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=({x}^{2}-2xy)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+({y}^{2}-2xy)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$\n\nIII. $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)={e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}(3y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}(3y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each vector field.\n\n- Vector field I doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2x+(-x)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II has a stream function. Indeed, the partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x-2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2y-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=(2x-2y)+(2y-2x)=0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III does have a stream function. Indeed, the partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=3{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-3{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=3{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y)+(-3{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y))=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491112 :question/problem "Calculate the slope of the line that passes through the points $(-2,-4)$ and $(-8,-6).$" :question/worked-solution "We let $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-2,-4)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(-8,-6),$ and compute the slope using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6-(-4)}{-8-(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the slope is $m=\\frac{1}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491125 :question/problem "![](images/c5/c5659998c906fd3bacea6186206b8670c6449e8700c211844f324a8aae0b84ed.png)\n\nWhat is the slope of the line shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The line is horizontal, and therefore it has a slope of zero.\n\nWe can see this by choosing two points on the line, such as $(0,4)$ and $(1,4),$ and computing the slope between them:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{4-4}{1-0}=\\frac{0}{1}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491128 :question/problem "What is the slope of a line that passes through the points $(0,3)$ and $(5,6)?$" :question/worked-solution "We let $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,3)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(5,6),$ and compute the slope using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-3}{5-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the slope is $m=\\frac{3}{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491141 :question/problem "![](images/10/10f79e3e129891e51ee47ecd595ab666053cd048688c4a63e8f7edd66cdae6da.png)\n\nGiven that the line above passes through the point $(-4,-1)$ and intersects the $x$-axis when $x=4,$ find the slope of the line." :question/worked-solution "Note that the line passes through the points $(-4,-1)$ and $(4,0).$ So, we let $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-4,-1)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(4,0),$ and compute the slope using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-(-1)}{4-(-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the line is $m=\\frac{1}{8}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491143 :question/problem "![](images/9b/9bcdefce22e090d5db2a0e75467b80c6f94ffa4133bb65cc21da44316d4a21bb.png)\n\nWhich of the following best describes the slope of the graph above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that slope is the amount the line rises vertically per unit that it runs horizontally.\n\nSince the graph increases ($↗$) as we go from left to right, the line rises by a positive amount.\n\nTherefore, the slope of the line is positive."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491175 :question/problem "![](images/1f/1f573f3cd0051a929088947685fbefb019e86a8d0f5e75f395106438aa04cfd2.png)\n\nWhich of the following best describes the slope of the graph above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that slope is the amount the line rises vertically per unit that it runs horizontally.\n\nSince the graph decreases ($↘$) as we go from left to right, the line rises by a negative amount.\n\nTherefore, the slope of the line is negative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491182 :question/problem "![](images/6c/6cc013b8a23067a1da77ec7a33a0be0d00d5d18ee1f4654e73acd5f388b76962.png)\n\nWhich of the following best describes the slope of the graph above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that slope is the amount the line rises vertically per unit that it runs horizontally.\n\nSince the graph decreases ($↘$) as we go from left to right, the line rises by a negative amount.\n\nTherefore, the slope of the line is negative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491185 :question/problem "Calculate the slope of the line that passes through the points $(3,4)$ and $(2,4).$" :question/worked-solution "We let $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(3,4)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(2,4),$ and compute the slope using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-4}{2-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{-1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the slope is $m=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491192 :question/problem "![](images/82/82df064eea5a3a2add91c3702154770ee66d208f28bfccbe49d4312a2d63d96b.png)\n\nWhich of the following best describes the slope of the graph above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that slope is the amount the line rises vertically per unit that it runs horizontally.\n\nSince the graph increases ($↗$) as we go from left to right, the line rises by a positive amount.\n\nTherefore, the slope of the line is positive."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491205 :question/problem "![](images/64/64957c4f5315d4880503aa6572699a675b4c16143ae08e58fb25df37fd69d05d.png)\n\nThe straight line shown above intersects the $y$-axis when $y=3$ and intersects the $x$-axis when $x=-6.$ What is its slope?" :question/worked-solution "Note that the line passes through the points $(0,3)$ and $(-6,0).$ So, we let $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,3)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(-6,0),$ and compute the slope using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-3}{-6-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the line is $m=\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491983 :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=2y-x,\\,v=y-x,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the lines\n\n$$\ny=1+\\frac{x}{2},\\,y=-1+\\frac{x}{2},\\,y=x-1,\\,y=x-2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=2y-x,\\,v=y-x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=2y-x$ into the equations of the first and second lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=1+\\frac{x}{2} & ⟹\\, & 2y-x=2 & ⟹\\, & u=2 \\\\  & y=-1+\\frac{x}{2} & ⟹\\, & 2y-x=-2 & ⟹\\, & u=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=y-x$ into the equations of the third and fourth lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=x-1 & ⟹\\, & y-x=-1 & ⟹\\, & v=-1 \\\\  & y=x-2 & ⟹\\, & y-x=-2 & ⟹\\, & v=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 2,\\,-2\\le v\\le -1\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/e9/e9ad855bcb5133e9a51d990ea25d0e23c8da88c59e15825f3ea1bfc47335dc33.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\text{Area}(Δ) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491993 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the following lines.\n\n$$\ny=2x-1,\\,y=2x-3,\\,x=2y+1,\\,x=2y+4\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\n2x-y=1,\\,2x-y=3,\\,x-2y=1,\\,x-2y=4\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu=2x-y,\\,v=x-2y\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=2x-y$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 2x-y=1\\,⟹\\,u=1 \\\\  & 2x-y=3\\,⟹\\,u=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=x-2y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & x-2y=1\\,⟹\\,v=1 \\\\  & x-2y=4\\,⟹\\,v=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,1\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/87/877dee1177d97f3c0df572e766d47cf31cec941d1770f72f5835433b386d5067.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-4+1 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{3}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{∬}_{Δ}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (3-1)\\cdot (4-1) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491995 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the following lines.\n\n$$\n2y=3x-2,\\,2y=3x-7,\\,2x=3y+1,\\,2x=3y+4\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\n3x-2y=2,\\,3x-2y=7,\\,2x-3y=1,\\,2x-3y=4\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu=3x-2y,\\,v=2x-3y\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=3x-2y$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 3x-2y=2\\,⟹\\,u=2 \\\\  & 3x-2y=7\\,⟹\\,u=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=2x-3y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 2x-3y=1\\,⟹\\,v=1 \\\\  & 2x-3y=4\\,⟹\\,v=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 7,\\,1\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/83/83798f4e941010a24a548d9403b7085fa8e4b8cca8dcb7c0ebc6670d4afbf517.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-9+4 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{5}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{∬}_{Δ}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{2}^{7}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (7-2)\\cdot (4-1) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491996 :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,v=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x},\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\ny={x}^{2},\\,y=3{x}^{2},\\,x={y}^{2},\\,x=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,v=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y={x}^{2} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}=1 & ⟹\\, & u=1 \\\\  & y=3{x}^{2} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}=3 & ⟹\\, & u=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & x={y}^{2} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}=1 & ⟹\\, & v=1 \\\\  & x=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}=4 & ⟹\\, & v=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,1\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/26/26eaba24af54a40f59f92148c145285ba4097ce765aee8cfc9a6241ff88b9498.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}} & \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} & \\text{ }\\text{ }\\frac{2y}{x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{{x}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{3{y}^{2}}=-\\frac{1}{3{u}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{3{u}^{2}}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3}+1)\\cdot (4-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492015 :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x+2y,\\,v=x,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(x+2y)^{2}-x-1=0$ and the line $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+2y,\\,v=x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $x+2y=u$ and $x=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2y)^{2}-x-1=0\\,⟹\\,{u}^{2}-v-1=0\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{2}-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $x=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=1\\,⟹\\,v=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &={u}^{2}-1 \\\\ v &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=\\pm \\sqrt{2} \\\\ v &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},1).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,{u}^{2}-1\\le v\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/0c/0c598c1c0d09fb26175cad17106bc4529165da619f35a5882ead0ba133d05c2c.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}={(-2)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}[{\\int }_{{u}^{2}-1}^{1}\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}[v]_{{u}^{2}-1}^{1}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}(1-({u}^{2}-1))du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}(2-{u}^{2})du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[2u-\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}]}_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((2\\sqrt{2}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3})-(-2\\sqrt{2}+\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3})) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492060 :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x-y,\\,v=y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $y+(x-y)^{4}=3$ and the line $y=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x-y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $x-y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+(x-y)^{4}=3\\,⟹\\,v+{u}^{4}=3\\,⟹\\,v=3-{u}^{4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=2\\,⟹\\,v=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &=3-{u}^{4} \\\\ v &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=\\pm 1 \\\\ v &=2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 1,2).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,2\\le v\\le 3-{u}^{4}\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/81/81ff220903cbe53cebf5e8f8b704e2a316be6092e1f14e527a7321e8f61bffb8.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|1|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[{\\int }_{2}^{3-{u}^{4}}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[v]_{2}^{3-{u}^{4}}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}((3-{u}^{4})-2)\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1-{u}^{4}))\\,du \\\\  & ={[u-\\frac{{u}^{5}}{5}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492063 :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y},\\,v=xy,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\ny={x}^{2},\\,y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3},\\,y=\\frac{2}{x},\\,y=\\frac{4}{x}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y},\\,v=xy\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y={x}^{2} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}=1 & ⟹\\, & u=1 \\\\  & y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}=3 & ⟹\\, & u=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=xy$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{2}{x} & ⟹\\, & xy=2 & ⟹\\, & v=2 \\\\  & y=\\frac{4}{x} & ⟹\\, & xy=4 & ⟹\\, & v=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,2\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/12/1207d8cdb8afd7c55d712caca043edfe9f0d0395343a6cbd329cf5861ec3bce7.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{2x}{y} & -\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ y & \\text{ }\\text{ }x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{y}{3{x}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{3u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{1}{3u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\cdot (4-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493330 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={(4x+6)}^{5},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=4x+6$ and $f(g(x))=g(x{)}^{5}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{5}{)}^{′}=5{g}^{4}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(4x+6{)}^{′}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =5{g}^{4}\\cdot (4) \\\\  & =20(4x+6{)}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493331 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\sqrt{5x-3},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=5x-3$ and $f(g(x))=\\sqrt{g(x)}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & ={(\\sqrt{g})}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{g}}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(5x-3)^{′}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{g}}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{5x-3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493332 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={({x}^{5}-7{x}^{4})}^{2},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=2({x}^{5}-7{x}^{4})($\n{{field-1}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)={x}^{5}-7{x}^{4}$ and $f(g(x))=g(x)^{2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{2})^{′}=2g, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{5}-7{x}^{4})^{′}=5{x}^{4}-28{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =2g\\cdot (5{x}^{4}-28{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =2({x}^{5}-7{x}^{4})(\\,5{x}^{4}-28{x}^{3}\\,).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493904 :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors $\\mathbf{a}=⟨-1,1,1⟩$, $\\mathbf{b}=⟨2,1,0⟩$, and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨k,-2,-3⟩$ are coplanar." :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ k & -2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}-1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ k & -3\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ k & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(1\\cdot (-3)-(-2)\\cdot 0)-(2\\cdot (-3)-k\\cdot 0)+(2\\cdot (-2)-k\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =3+6-4-k \\\\  & =5-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =0 \\\\ 5-k & =0 \\\\ k & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493949 :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors $\\mathbf{x}=⟨1,k,2⟩$, $\\mathbf{y}=⟨5,-2,3⟩$, and $\\mathbf{z}=⟨-4,k,1⟩$ are coplanar.\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (y\\times z) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & k & 2 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -4 & k & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ k & 1\\end{vmatrix}-k\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ -4 & k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-2\\cdot 1-3\\cdot k)-k(5\\cdot 1-3\\cdot -4)+2(5k-(-2)(-4)) \\\\  & =-2-3k-k(5+12)+2(5k-8) \\\\  & =-2-3k-17k+10k-16 \\\\  & =-10k-18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (y\\times z) & =0 \\\\ -10k-18 & =0 \\\\ k & =-\\frac{9}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-\\frac{9}{5}.$"}]}
